  "page1": {
    "title": "Reading Data at a Glance",
    "subtitle": "How ratio graphs turn a pile of numbers into one clear picture.",
    "body": "Our class voted for one favorite sport. Twelve students said soccer, and twelve said something else. Soccer got half of all the votes, so its ratio was 50 percent.\n\nNumbers in a long list are hard to read. A ratio graph shows each part as a slice of the whole. We can <mark>see it at a glance</mark>.\n\nIn a circle graph, the whole circle is one hundred percent. Because soccer was half, it fills half the circle. A bar graph does the same job in a straight strip.\n\nThe biggest part jumps out first. Smaller parts sit beside it. When the slices grow or shrink, the ratios change too.\n\nSo a good graph lets numbers speak in shapes.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/18.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A class survey shown as a circle graph and a bar graph, with soccer filling half of each.",
